// F200, Group C of the Map/Set pairing pass. Seven names settled, on three decisions the user
// made (2026-10-20):
//
//   1. `merge`, not `set_all`, for pouring one map into another -- the established immutable
//      name, where `set_all` reads like a bulk `set`, which it is not.
//   2. `map` over a Map takes `fn(K, V) -> R`, not `fn(V) -> R`. It is the key-aware form, which
//      matches `filter`'s `fn(K, V) -> bool`, and it replaces the stuttering `map_map_values`.
//   3. `remove` answers the value it took out; `discard` answers nothing. Both kept -- the value
//      is wanted about half the time, and building an `Option` to throw away is what `discard`
//      exists to avoid. `discard` answered a `bool` before this.
//
// **Decision 3 was reversed on 2026-10-22**, and both halves of it are kept above because the
// reversal is the interesting part. `remove` went variadic to absorb `remove_all`, and a variadic
// form has no single value to answer, so it answers `bool` now. The premise that went was "the
// value is wanted about half the time": measured across lib/, tests/ and the sample backend, it
// was wanted **nowhere** -- the only two readers were `remove_all` and `remove_keys`, each using
// it as a presence test with the binding discarded.
//
// That leaves `discard` with nothing to avoid. The two calls at the bottom of this file --
// `remove(mm, "a")` discarded and `discard(mm, "b")` -- are now the same operation at the same
// cost, which is the clearest place in the tree to see that the pair has collapsed into one. Left
// standing rather than deleted in the same breath, because keeping or dropping `discard` is its
// own decision.
fn main() {
    val m = Map<string, int> { "a": 1, "b": 2 }

    print(merge(m, Map<string, int> { "c": 3 }))
    val mm = mutable(m)
    merge(mm, Map<string, int> { "c": 3 })
    merge(mm, mutable(Map<string, int> { "d": 4 }))
    print(snapshot(mm))

    print(entries(m))
    print(entries(mm))

    print(map<string, int, string>(m, fn(k, v) { concat(k, str(v)) }))
    print(map<int, int>(Set<int> { 1, 2 }, fn(x) { x * 10 }))

    set(mm, "z", 9)
    print(get(mm, "z"))

    // the pair decision 3 settles: one answers, one does not, and discarding the answer of the
    // one that does is the ordinary call rather than a mistake
    print(remove(mm, "z"))
    remove(mm, "a")
    discard(mm, "b")
    print(snapshot(mm))
}
